From c5a32feb4acd8762e7be47a6836513c3003c29af Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Theorem Labs
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 06:04:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [sanitizer_common] intercept qsort_r on musl
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

🤖: Patch 0006 moved the interceptor gates that asked about glibc where musl
has the function too, but it left SANITIZER_INTERCEPT_QSORT_R at SI_GLIBC.
musl has had qsort_r since 1.2.3, with glibc's argument order: the
comparator's third argument is the caller's pointer.  src/stdlib/qsort.c
defines __qsort_r and exports it with weak_alias(__qsort_r, qsort_r), at
lines 166 and 229 of the pinned base f21a9653.  With the gate at SI_GLIBC no
qsort_r interceptor is compiled, so qsort_r is not in the wrap list.  A static
musl program's qsort_r call then goes straight to libc.  It does not get the
interceptor's comparator pass over the array, or its
COMMON_INTERCEPTOR_WRITE_RANGE check of all nmemb * size bytes, as the same
call does under glibc ASan.

Check musl as well.  gen_wrap_list.py takes the names that libc.a defines,
weak ones included (nm --defined-only --extern-only), so qsort_r joins the
wrap list, and REAL(qsort_r) is musl's qsort_r.

This also changes qsort on musl.  With SANITIZER_INTERCEPT_QSORT_R set,
sanitizer_common_interceptors.inc builds the qsort interceptor that calls
WRAP(qsort_r) with a null argument.  Until now it built the stand-alone one,
which keeps the comparator in two thread-local variables and calls
REAL(qsort).  glibc builds already use the WRAP(qsort_r) version.  musl's own
qsort is __qsort_r with a comparator adapter (src/stdlib/qsort_nr.c), so
REAL(qsort_r) sorts as REAL(qsort) did, and a qsort call is checked in the
same way as before: one comparator pass, then one WRITE_RANGE of the array.

sanitizer_platform_interceptors.h is a shared header.  The runtimes that
include sanitizer_common_interceptors.inc and keep this gate change: ASan (the
archive and its wrap list), MSan (the archive and its wrap list) and TSan.
HWASan sets the gate to 0 itself, and standalone UBSan does not include the
.inc.  A toolchain
that has this patch is a new toolchain version.  Its libclang_rt.asan.a is not
the archive that the earlier binaries linked.

Reported in theorem-labs/asan-static#1.

Omp-Session: 01a175dd-5d8a-75ac-99a0-68919637ef66
---
 .../lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_platform_interceptors.h     | 3 ++-
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_platform_interceptors.h b/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_platform_interceptors.h
index a90b5ff..04b2f27 100644
--- a/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_platform_interceptors.h
+++ b/compiler-rt/lib/sanitizer_common/sanitizer_platform_interceptors.h
@@ -628,7 +628,8 @@ SANITIZER_WEAK_IMPORT void *aligned_alloc(__sanitizer::usize __alignment,
    SI_FREEBSD || SI_SOLARIS)
 #define SANITIZER_INTERCEPT_QSORT \
   (SI_POSIX && !SI_IOSSIM && !SI_WATCHOS && !SI_TVOS && !SI_ANDROID)
-#define SANITIZER_INTERCEPT_QSORT_R SI_GLIBC
+// musl has had qsort_r, with glibc's argument order, since 1.2.3.
+#define SANITIZER_INTERCEPT_QSORT_R (SI_GLIBC || SANITIZER_MUSL)
 #define SANITIZER_INTERCEPT_BSEARCH \
   (SI_POSIX && !SI_IOSSIM && !SI_WATCHOS && !SI_TVOS && !SI_ANDROID)
 // sigaltstack on i386 macOS cannot be intercepted due to setjmp()
